1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,568
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,588 --> 00:00:07,494
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:07,894 --> 00:00:13,034
This is Lecture 7, thread tension and stitch quality.

4
00:00:13,434 --> 00:00:18,204
The needle thread pulls from above, the bobbin thread from below,

5
00:00:18,224 --> 00:00:23,658
and the point where they lock should sit right in the middle of the cloth.

6
00:00:23,678 --> 00:00:28,817
Pull it off-centre and you get bobbin dots on top or loops underneath;

7
00:00:28,837 --> 00:00:32,428
pull both too hard and the stitch looks balanced,

8
00:00:32,448 --> 00:00:35,597
yet the seam puckers and the thread breaks.

9
00:00:37,117 --> 00:00:39,123
Here's the problem.

10
00:00:39,523 --> 00:00:47,712
A shirt factory is sewing plackets: two plies of cotton, 1 millimetre thick,

11
00:00:47,732 --> 00:00:51,708
a 3 millimetre stitch, Tex 24 thread.

12
00:00:52,108 --> 00:00:58,544
A new mechanic thinks the stitches aren't tight enough and sets the tension

13
00:00:58,564 --> 00:01:02,848
assembly to 220 centinewtons and the bobbin to 90.

14
00:01:03,248 --> 00:01:06,489
The stitch looks balanced on both faces,

15
00:01:06,509 --> 00:01:11,624
but the finished placket puckers in fine ridges along the seam.

16
00:01:12,024 --> 00:01:14,364
Is it really balanced?

17
00:01:14,764 --> 00:01:18,703
How much shorter is a 40 centimetre placket?

18
00:01:19,103 --> 00:01:21,363
How do we get it balanced and flat?

19
00:01:21,763 --> 00:01:23,183
We'll work it out at the end.

20
00:01:24,703 --> 00:01:29,690
Looking at a seam, a mechanic checks four things: bobbin dots showing on top,

21
00:01:29,710 --> 00:01:34,307
loops of needle thread trailing underneath, whether the seam lies flat,

22
00:01:34,327 --> 00:01:36,843
and whether the thread breaks or frays.

23
00:01:37,243 --> 00:01:44,868
This is the stitch cross-section from Lab 7-1: blue is the needle thread,

24
00:01:44,888 --> 00:01:50,943
orange the bobbin, and the white dots are where they lock.

25
00:01:51,343 --> 00:01:57,885
As the tension assembly tightens and loosens, the lock point runs up and down.

26
00:01:58,285 --> 00:02:04,330
The first two faults are a wrong ratio, the third usually both too tight,

27
00:02:04,350 --> 00:02:07,156
the fourth a peak that's too high.

28
00:02:08,675 --> 00:02:10,052
Where tension comes from.

29
00:02:11,572 --> 00:02:14,752
First, where does tension come from?

30
00:02:15,152 --> 00:02:19,012
This is the take-up animation from Lecture 4.

31
00:02:19,412 --> 00:02:25,066
The needle thread leaves the tension assembly, passes the check spring,

32
00:02:25,086 --> 00:02:31,059
wraps round several guides, the take-up lever eye and the needle-bar guide,

33
00:02:31,079 --> 00:02:34,256
and finally goes through the needle eye.

34
00:02:34,656 --> 00:02:38,534
The bobbin thread is much simpler: drawn off the bobbin,

35
00:02:38,554 --> 00:02:43,476
it runs under a leaf spring on the case, and a screw sets the pressure.

36
00:02:43,876 --> 00:02:49,267
Every part on the needle-thread path changes the tension:

37
00:02:49,287 --> 00:02:55,913
the assembly adds a fixed amount, the guides multiply it, and the eye,

38
00:02:55,933 --> 00:03:01,894
where the thread turns about 180 degrees, cuts a big piece off.

39
00:03:03,414 --> 00:03:10,191
The tension assembly is a stud, two discs, a compression spring and a nut.

40
00:03:10,591 --> 00:03:18,152
The needle thread runs between the discs; the spring presses them together,

41
00:03:18,172 --> 00:03:23,105
and when the thread is pulled both its faces rub,

42
00:03:23,125 --> 00:03:27,452
so the tension rises by 2 times mu times N.

43
00:03:27,852 --> 00:03:31,872
That's a fixed amount added, whatever comes in.

44
00:03:32,272 --> 00:03:37,851
Tighten the nut, N goes up, and the thread gets tighter.

45
00:03:38,251 --> 00:03:46,358
A number: 10 centinewtons in, a 200 centinewton spring, mu of 0.2,

46
00:03:46,378 --> 00:03:48,452
and 90 comes out.

47
00:03:48,852 --> 00:03:51,216
When you lift the presser foot,

48
00:03:51,236 --> 00:03:55,370
the discs are pushed at least half a millimetre apart,

49
00:03:55,390 --> 00:03:57,754
so the thread pulls out freely.

50
00:03:59,274 --> 00:04:05,344
When thread wraps round a cylindrical part such as a guide,

51
00:04:05,364 --> 00:04:10,918
friction no longer adds a fixed amount but multiplies:

52
00:04:10,938 --> 00:04:14,737
T2 equals T1 times e to the mu theta.

53
00:04:15,137 --> 00:04:17,952
That's the capstan equation.

54
00:04:18,352 --> 00:04:24,303
Mu 0.2 over 1 radian gives a factor of 1.22;

55
00:04:24,323 --> 00:04:29,052
mu 0.35 over 180 degrees gives 3.0.

56
00:04:29,452 --> 00:04:31,327
Read it the other way too:

57
00:04:31,347 --> 00:04:36,792
thread always slides from its low-tension end towards its high-tension end.

58
00:04:37,192 --> 00:04:42,702
When the stitch is tightened, the lock point is the upstream end,

59
00:04:42,722 --> 00:04:46,955
so the take-up tension, after the turn at the eye,

60
00:04:46,975 --> 00:04:49,932
arrives there cut to about a third.

61
00:04:51,452 --> 00:04:56,098
Needle-thread tension isn't constant within a stitch.

62
00:04:56,498 --> 00:05:02,407
A study put a tension sensor just after the tension assembly and found this pattern:

63
00:05:02,427 --> 00:05:06,713
the highest peak comes at the moment the stitch is tightened,

64
00:05:06,733 --> 00:05:10,878
and it's controlled directly by the assembly's pre-tension.

65
00:05:11,278 --> 00:05:16,733
Then the take-up pays out and the check spring keeps a small tension;

66
00:05:16,753 --> 00:05:22,526
after the catch the loop passes round the bobbin case and gives a second,

67
00:05:22,546 --> 00:05:23,558
smaller peak.

68
00:05:23,958 --> 00:05:26,552
Fabric thickness hardly matters,

69
00:05:26,572 --> 00:05:30,556
and the bobbin only plays a part around cast-off.

70
00:05:30,956 --> 00:05:32,706
The practical point:

71
00:05:32,726 --> 00:05:38,016
turning the tension nut mainly changes that tightening peak.

72
00:05:39,536 --> 00:05:40,968
Stitch balance.

73
00:05:42,488 --> 00:05:44,387
As the stitch is tightened,

74
00:05:44,407 --> 00:05:48,865
the take-up pulls the needle thread up while the bobbin thread,

75
00:05:48,885 --> 00:05:52,348
drawn out of the case, pulls the lock point down.

76
00:05:52,748 --> 00:05:54,088
It's a tug of war.

77
00:05:54,488 --> 00:05:55,806
Watch the section:

78
00:05:55,826 --> 00:06:00,341
tighten the assembly and the lock point is pulled to the top,

79
00:06:00,361 --> 00:06:03,315
and orange bobbin dots show on the face;

80
00:06:03,335 --> 00:06:09,188
loosen it and the point runs to the bottom and needle thread trails underneath.

81
00:06:09,588 --> 00:06:14,206
Only when the two are roughly equal does it sit in the middle.

82
00:06:14,606 --> 00:06:17,329
That's a balanced stitch.

83
00:06:17,729 --> 00:06:20,170
They needn't be exactly equal,

84
00:06:20,190 --> 00:06:24,272
because the threads turn at the edges of the hole,

85
00:06:24,292 --> 00:06:27,389
and friction there works like a brake.

86
00:06:28,909 --> 00:06:32,754
The book puts numbers on this with an illustrative model.

87
00:06:33,154 --> 00:06:39,572
The lock-point position z runs from 0 at the bottom of the cloth to 1 at the top,

88
00:06:39,592 --> 00:06:45,614
and r is the needle tension at the lock point divided by the bobbin tension.

89
00:06:46,014 --> 00:06:51,490
At r equal to 1, z is 0.5, right in the middle;

90
00:06:51,510 --> 00:06:58,506
at r equal to the balance band B, the point reaches the top.

91
00:06:58,906 --> 00:07:04,160
Between 0.4 and 0.6 counts as balanced.

92
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:11,941
Look at the three curves: denim, with B of 3, is flat and easy to set; lining,

93
00:07:11,961 --> 00:07:18,772
with B of 1.8, is steep, and a small error sends the point to a surface.

94
00:07:19,172 --> 00:07:23,432
The straight-line relation is only a teaching simplification.

95
00:07:24,952 --> 00:07:30,176
Everyone on the shop floor knows the assembly must be several times tighter

96
00:07:30,196 --> 00:07:32,834
than the bobbin for a balanced stitch.

97
00:07:33,234 --> 00:07:33,501
Why?

98
00:07:33,901 --> 00:07:37,925
The model compares the needle tension at the lock point.

99
00:07:38,325 --> 00:07:41,176
From the assembly to that point,

100
00:07:41,196 --> 00:07:46,199
the thread is first multiplied by 1.22 round the guides,

101
00:07:46,219 --> 00:07:53,105
then cut to a third at the eye; together, about 0.41 of the assembly setting.

102
00:07:53,505 --> 00:08:01,919
So with a 40 centinewton bobbin, r equal to 1 needs the assembly at about 98,

103
00:08:01,939 --> 00:08:07,285
and the cotton balance zone is roughly 84 to 115.

104
00:08:08,805 --> 00:08:14,986
This is the tension plane from Lab 7-1: across is the assembly tension,

105
00:08:15,006 --> 00:08:17,169
up is the bobbin tension.

106
00:08:17,569 --> 00:08:22,549
Green is usable: balanced, firm, flat and unbroken.

107
00:08:22,949 --> 00:08:30,509
Yellow is unbalanced, grey too loose, red puckered, and purple a breakage risk.

108
00:08:30,909 --> 00:08:33,794
The white ring is the current setting,

109
00:08:33,814 --> 00:08:36,929
100 on the assembly and 40 on the bobbin.

110
00:08:37,329 --> 00:08:43,075
On the right, cotton has a fairly wide green band; on the left, thin,

111
00:08:43,095 --> 00:08:47,003
slippery lining has a narrow band set low down,

112
00:08:47,023 --> 00:08:50,429
and a little extra tension brings pucker.

113
00:08:51,949 --> 00:08:54,326
Order of adjustment and diagnosis.

114
00:08:55,846 --> 00:09:02,479
The rule is bobbin first, then the needle thread to match, because the bobbin,

115
00:09:02,499 --> 00:09:06,146
set by its spring, is the stable reference.

116
00:09:06,546 --> 00:09:10,213
Look at the section: this machine has a loose bobbin,

117
00:09:10,233 --> 00:09:15,082
the lock point is pulled to the top, and bobbin dots show on the face.

118
00:09:15,102 --> 00:09:20,161
r is too large, which could mean a tight needle thread or a loose bobbin.

119
00:09:20,561 --> 00:09:26,334
If the assembly is at its usual setting and the machine suddenly does this,

120
00:09:26,354 --> 00:09:28,883
the bobbin is the likely culprit,

121
00:09:28,903 --> 00:09:33,054
such as a loose case spring or a loosely wound bobbin,

122
00:09:33,074 --> 00:09:36,221
and loosening the assembly only hides it.

123
00:09:36,621 --> 00:09:40,098
Loops underneath mean r is too small,

124
00:09:40,118 --> 00:09:47,281
or the thread isn't seated between the discs, so thread it with the foot up.

125
00:09:48,801 --> 00:09:50,598
Pucker and breakage.

126
00:09:52,118 --> 00:09:55,976
Balance says the ratio is right, not the size.

127
00:09:56,376 --> 00:10:02,373
A thread maker's guidance sorts seam pucker into four types.

128
00:10:02,773 --> 00:10:07,793
Tension pucker: the thread is stretched as the stitch tightens,

129
00:10:07,813 --> 00:10:12,673
springs back once sewn, and shortens the cloth so it buckles;

130
00:10:12,693 --> 00:10:16,753
the cure is the lowest tension that still balances.

131
00:10:17,153 --> 00:10:23,875
Structural pucker: in a tight weave, needle and thread push the yarns apart.

132
00:10:24,275 --> 00:10:28,565
Dimensional instability: after washing and pressing,

133
00:10:28,585 --> 00:10:32,295
thread and cloth shrink by different amounts.

134
00:10:32,695 --> 00:10:38,115
Feed pucker is the ply shift of Lecture 6.

135
00:10:38,515 --> 00:10:42,115
The first type is the one this lecture is about.

136
00:10:43,635 --> 00:10:46,672
Tension pucker can be estimated.

137
00:10:47,072 --> 00:10:56,077
The thread's stretch during tightening is about the tension over its stiffness EA,

138
00:10:56,097 --> 00:11:01,800
and EA is roughly strength over elongation at break,

139
00:11:01,820 --> 00:11:05,652
about 6700 centinewtons for Tex 24.

140
00:11:06,052 --> 00:11:15,512
Thread used per unit of seam is about s plus t over s, here 1.33.

141
00:11:15,912 --> 00:11:21,479
Multiply the two and you get the fractional shortening of the seam;

142
00:11:21,499 --> 00:11:25,732
the cloth absorbs a little, and the rest is pucker.

143
00:11:26,132 --> 00:11:30,750
Watch the section: both threads tightened in proportion,

144
00:11:30,770 --> 00:11:36,712
the lock point stays in the middle, yet the cloth puckers more and more.

145
00:11:38,232 --> 00:11:40,584
Now breakage.

146
00:11:40,984 --> 00:11:45,197
The needle thread usually breaks at the tightening peak,

147
00:11:45,217 --> 00:11:49,882
and what it can bear is far below its tensile-tester strength:

148
00:11:49,902 --> 00:11:52,452
high-speed passes through the eye,

149
00:11:52,472 --> 00:11:56,684
rubbing on the hook and needle heat all take their toll.

150
00:11:57,084 --> 00:12:04,304
The illustrative rule: the peak should stay under a third of nominal strength.

151
00:12:04,704 --> 00:12:12,382
Four plies of denim need 100 centinewtons in both threads at the lock point,

152
00:12:12,402 --> 00:12:19,270
so the assembly is about 246, and at 5000 rpm the peak is about 345:

153
00:12:19,290 --> 00:12:24,740
35 percent of Tex 24, too much, hence all that purple.

154
00:12:25,140 --> 00:12:28,520
With Tex 40 it's 20 percent.

155
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:33,420
Heavy cloth needs heavy thread.

156
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:39,218
Robots use the capstan equation every day.

157
00:12:39,618 --> 00:12:44,230
Many dexterous hands and soft arms are tendon-driven:

158
00:12:44,250 --> 00:12:50,610
a motor in the palm or forearm pulls a cable that wraps round pulleys and

159
00:12:50,630 --> 00:12:54,718
bends through the joints to pull the fingertip.

160
00:12:55,118 --> 00:13:01,280
That's the same problem as the needle thread running from the assembly to the

161
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:02,163
lock point.

162
00:13:02,563 --> 00:13:07,166
With a total wrap of 270 degrees and mu of 0.1,

163
00:13:07,186 --> 00:13:13,067
the motor end pulls 1.6 times the fingertip; when releasing,

164
00:13:13,087 --> 00:13:16,903
the ratio flips and you get hysteresis.

165
00:13:17,303 --> 00:13:21,520
So a tension sensor near the joint closes the loop,

166
00:13:21,540 --> 00:13:26,172
just like electronic thread tension on a sewing machine,

167
00:13:26,192 --> 00:13:29,911
where a solenoid current sets the disc force.

168
00:13:31,431 --> 00:13:35,393
An everyday example: a mooring bollard.

169
00:13:35,793 --> 00:13:40,839
A sailor wraps the line two or three times round the bollard,

170
00:13:40,859 --> 00:13:43,413
and one person can hold a ship:

171
00:13:43,433 --> 00:13:48,893
every extra turn lets friction multiply the tension several times.

172
00:13:49,293 --> 00:13:52,109
That's the capstan equation.

173
00:13:52,509 --> 00:13:58,871
The needle thread turning about 180 degrees at the eye and losing two thirds

174
00:13:58,891 --> 00:14:03,070
of its tension is the same law used the other way:

175
00:14:03,090 --> 00:14:09,117
the ship's end is the take-up lever, the sailor's end is the lock point.

176
00:14:10,637 --> 00:14:11,777
Back to the start.

177
00:14:12,177 --> 00:14:22,344
With the assembly at 220, the needle tension at the lock point is about 89;

178
00:14:22,364 --> 00:14:30,357
against the 90 centinewton bobbin, r is 0.99 and z is 0.50.

179
00:14:30,757 --> 00:14:32,417
It really is balanced.

180
00:14:32,817 --> 00:14:40,599
But the two threads average about 90, so the seam shortens 1.8 percent;

181
00:14:40,619 --> 00:14:47,961
less the 0.6 percent the cotton absorbs, that's 1.2 percent pucker,

182
00:14:47,981 --> 00:14:51,917
4.8 millimetres over 40 centimetres.

183
00:14:52,317 --> 00:14:53,337
That's the ridge.

184
00:14:53,737 --> 00:15:01,654
Loosen both in proportion, to 100 and 40: r is 1.02, still balanced,

185
00:15:01,674 --> 00:15:06,557
pucker 0.21 percent, only 0.8 millimetres.

186
00:15:06,957 --> 00:15:07,878
You can't see it.

187
00:15:09,398 --> 00:15:14,272
